#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:27
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:28
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This module allows you to control the settings of the <application>"
"Uncomplicated FireWall</application> (UFW), which is a command line frontend "
"to "
"the default &Linux; firewall (IP-Tables)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:32
#, no-c-format
msgid "How Firewall Works"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:33
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As firewalling is a really complex topic, I'd like to give you a briefly "
"introduction about what firewalls are and how they work."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:36
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you think of a firewall most people would say <quote>"
"firewalls just restrict the network traffic</quote>, but a modern firewall "
"has "
"more to do that just restricting the traffic (which is, of course, it's main "
"task)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:40
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can filter by having a look at the packet headers that store information "
"about where the packet comes from, which protocol is used etc."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:42
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The second important feature a firewall has to provide is the possibility to "
"log the network traffic. This is a much more important part of it than you "
"may "
"think. Logfiles are give you a good view about the activities in your "
"network, "
"like who did what and when."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:45
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Logs are also really important when the firewall got cracked and you want to "
"know how. In that you would have a much harder life if you hadn't activated "
"the "
"logging functions so that you can see what has happened and how the "
"configuration could be improved."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:48
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Most people think about firewalls as just being used to block traffic from "
"the "
"Internet to your host, but you may also configure your firewall to restrict "
"the "
"Internet access. For example you may block some well known porn sites on your "
"Children's PC, or you just want to allow email and Web browsing in your "
"office "
"but you don't want your employees to be able to download files via &FTP;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:50
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"All the things described are really easy to implement with UFW once you have "
"understood how it works."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:56
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid "Default Policies"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:59
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"UFW can be configured as to what the default policy should be for incoming, "
"or "
"outgoing traffic. After you set the default policy, you can provide "
"exceptions "
"for it with custom rules. These default policies may be set to:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:61
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Allow</quote> - accepts connections to or from the computer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:62
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Deny</quote> - blocks access to or from the computer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:63 index.docbook:115
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Reject</quote> - similar to Deny, but lets the sender know when "
"traffic "
"is being denied, rather than simply ignoring it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid "IPv6 Support"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:70
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To enable support for rules based upon IPv6 addresses, you should enabled the "
"<quote>IPv6 support</quote> setting."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:74
#, no-c-format
msgid "Logging"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The general logging of the firewall may be set using the <guilabel>"
"Logging level</guilabel> setting. This can be set to one of the following "
"values:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Off</quote> - disables logging."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Low</quote> - logs all blocked packets not matching the default policy "
"(limiting the number of duplicate access attempts logged), as well as packets "
"matching logged rules."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:82
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Medium</quote> - as per <quote>Low</quote>, plus all allowed packets "
"not "
"matching the default policy, all invalid packets, and all new connections. "
"All "
"logging is done limiting the number of duplicate access attempts logged."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:84
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>High</quote> - as per <quote>Medium</quote> (without limiting), plus "
"all "
"packets with (with limiting)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Full</quote> - log everything, without limiting."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:89
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Levels above <quote>Medium</quote> generate a lot of logging output, and may "
"quickly fill up your disk. Medium may generate a lot of logging output on a "
"busy system."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid "Rules"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:98
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"UFW functions by being provided a list of rules as to which ports/services "
"show "
"be allowed or blocked. Rules provide exceptions to the default policies."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:99
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For example, you may want to configure your computer to not be accessible "
"from "
"the Internet in case it connects directly to the world wide web with a public "
"IP address, but you may want the computer to be open to all connections from "
"local intranets and also to allow for ssh connection from anywhere (both "
"local "
"and internet). In such a case you will want to set default policy to 'deny', "
"but then add rules to allow incoming ssh connections and connections from the "
"default local network address spaces."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:100
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Rule ordering is important and the first match wins. Therefore when adding "
"rules, add the more specific rules first with more general rules later. Rules "
"may be re-ordered via the <guibutton>Move Up</guibutton> / <guibutton>"
"Move Down</guibutton> buttons, or by drag-n-drop of the entries in the rules "
"list."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:102
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding and Editing Rules"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are two <quote>modes</quote> to add, and edit, rules:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:105
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Simple</quote> - allows controlling of arbitrary port numbers. These "
"may "
"be set via a list of predefined services/applications, or entered manually."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Advanced</quote> - allows complete control of source/destination "
"address "
"and port numbers. Only the fields that you require need to be filled in."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:109
#, no-c-format
msgid "The following is a list of features that are common to both:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:111
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Policy</quote> controls how connections are handled."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Allow</quote> - accepts access to the specified ports/services."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:114
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Deny</quote> - blocks access to the specified ports/services."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:117
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Limit</quote> - enables connection rate limiting. This is useful for "
"protecting against brute-force login attacks. The firewall will deny "
"connections if an IP address has attempted to initiate 6 or more connections "
"in "
"the last 30 seconds"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:122
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Direction</quote> - indicates the direction of the connection."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:123
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Port</quote> - this is used to define the port, or ports, to be "
"controlled. There are two options for this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Manually entering a port number. There is a text field that allows you to "
"enter "
"one, or more, port numbers - or a range of port numbers. (Ranges are of the "
"form <quote>from:to</quote>, &eg; <quote>123:456</quote>)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:126
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Below the text field, there is a drop down box containing a set of "
"pre-defined "
"services, applications, and UFW application profile names."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:129
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Protocol</quote> - sets the type of port that will be controlled. "
"(This "
"setting is only applicable when the port number is to be entered manually)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: quote
#: index.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Any</quote>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: quote
#: index.docbook:132
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>TCP</quote>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: quote
#: index.docbook:133
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>UDP</quote>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:136
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Logging</quote> - controls per-rule logging"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:138
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>None</quote> - no logging is performed when a packet matches a rule."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:139
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>New connections</quote> - will log all new connections matching a rule."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:140
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>All packets</quote> - will log all packets matching a rule."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:143
#, no-c-format
msgid "<quote>Description</quote> - a textual description of the rule."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:146
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <quote>Advanced</quote> mode will allow you to specify any combination "
"of; "
"source address, source port, destination address, and destination port - as "
"well as specifying the network interface (&eg; wlan0, eth0, &etc;) to apply "
"the "
"rule on. Addresses may be either; an IP address (&eg; 192.168.1.100), or "
"subnet "
"(&eg; 192.168.1.0/24)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:149
#, no-c-format
msgid "IPv6 Rules"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:150
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When IPv6 support is enabled, there are in effect two firewalls - one for "
"IPv4 "
"connections, and one for IPv6 connections. This may cause some unexpected "
"behavior."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:154
#, no-c-format
msgid "Editing"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:155
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Each time you create a rule the firewall may create a corresponding IPv6 "
"rule. "
"&eg; if you create a rule to block port 22 (and do not specify an IPv4 "
"address), the firewall will create two rules - one for IPv4 connections, and "
"one for IPv6 connections. If you now edit the IPv4 version of this rule, and "
"change its port number, then the IPv4 rule will be updated, a new IPv6 rule "
"will be created, and the old IPv6 rule will still exist. Therefore, after "
"editing the IPv4 rule you would need to delete the old IPv6 rule."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid "Ordering"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:165
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the list of rules, IPv6 rules are always grouped after the IPv4 rules, and "
"it is not possible to re-order an IPv6 rule to be before an IPv4 rule. This "
"is "
"because the IPv6 rules are only applied to IPv6 connections, and therefore, "
"intermingling them with IPv4 rules makes no sense."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:175
#, no-c-format
msgid "Modules"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:176
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"UFW supports loading of IP-Tables kernel modules to improve its "
"functionality. "
"The currently supported modules on your system will be listed in the <"
"guilabel>"
"Modules</guilabel> section. Each enabled module is indicated with a checkmark."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:178
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are two types of modules:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:180
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>Connection tracking</quote> - is the ability to maintain connection "
"state information (such as source and destination address/port, protocol, "
"etc.) "
"in memory. Using these modules makes the firewall more secure."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:183
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<quote>NAT (Network Address Translation)</quote> - Where any form of NAT "
"(SNAT, "
"DNAT, Masquerading) on your firewall is involved, some commands and responses "
"may also need to be modified by the firewall. This is the job of the NAT "
"modules. NOTE: If you are using the corresponding Connection Tracking module, "
"then you should also enable the module."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:193
#, no-c-format
msgid "Profiles"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:194
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The current configuration of your firewall may be saved in a <quote>"
"profile.</quote> These will contain your rules, the default settings, and the "
"list of active IP-Tables modules. Using profiles allows you to easily switch "
"between firewall settings."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:196
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The profile functions can be accessed via the menu button in the lower-right "
"of "
"the main window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:200
#, no-c-format
msgid "Log Viewer"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:201
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have enabled logging, then the firewall will log any connections to "
"the "
"system log file. To view these entries, a simple log viewer is included."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:203
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The log viewer is activated via the menu button in the lower-right of the "
"main "
"window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:207
#, no-c-format
msgid "Example: configuring a firewall for your computer"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:210
#, no-c-format
msgid "Describing the situation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:212
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Let us imagine you have a workstation computer at home that is connected into "
"a "
"closed network with your neighbours, maybe 15 computers in all. You do not "
"know "
"if what neighbours are using as their primary OS'es, you also are not sure "
"that "
"their computers are well attended, virus and spyware free. So, though your "
"network is behind a router with a firewall, you do not trust your network too "
"much. You do not run any server or anything like that on your workstation, "
"but "
"sometimes you send and get files via bittorrent network using KTorrent. To "
"make "
"that process efficient, you have forwarded the router ports 6881/TCP and "
"4444/UDP to your workstation. You also connect to your workstation from "
"outside "
"using ssh, and to make that possible, the router is forwarding to your "
"workstation the port 22. To make the forwarding possible, the router reserves "
"to your workstation IP address 192.168.1.122."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:214
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You also have a laptop that you connect to this network once in a while, but "
"you also connect to other networks and to broadband at your mother's house. "
"So "
"it needs to be flexible about network configuration possibilities and secure "
"at "
"the same time. When you are connected to the home network, the router gives "
"your notebook a reserved address 192.168.1.111."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:216
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You use Dropbox on your workstation and your laptop to sync files. You also "
"share files using Samba (&Windows; networking). You want both your computers "
"to "
"have samba shares open to your friend, whose computer has a static IP address "
"192.168.1.133."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:220
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuring"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:223
#, no-c-format
msgid "Setting general policies"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:225
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"So, how do you go about figuring your firewall, if you need to stay secure, "
"but "
"still be able to use KTorrent, Dropbox, ssh, and Samba?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:227
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"First of all, you will need to set a default policy preventing other hosts "
"access to any services on your computers."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:229
#, no-c-format
msgid "This is common to both of your computers, so let us do it now:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literallayout
#: index.docbook:231
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"- tick the tickbox \"Firewall status\" to enable firewall;\n"
"- set \"Default outgoing policy\" to \"Allow\";\n"
"- set \"Default incoming policy\" to \"Deny\";\n"
"- set \"Logging level\", \"Low\", unless you know why you need a different "
"level."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:238
#, no-c-format
msgid "General configuration settings"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:243
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"After setting the general policy, you will need to make some exceptions to "
"this "
"policy. To enable access to Samba and ssh services on your computers, you "
"will "
"need ssh and Samba ports to be opened. In order to use Dropbox and KTorrent "
"you "
"generally do not need any ports to be opened, but it can help a great deal, "
"if "
"you are using lansync on Dropbox (and you will, since you synchronize files "
"between the workstation and laptop, and it is much quicker using lansync), "
"and "
"if you want better rating on bittorrent network while sharing files with "
"KTorrent."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:245
#, no-c-format
msgid "Let us start with your workstation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:247
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On the workstation we need to open access to ssh, Dropbox lansync, Samba, and "
"KTorrent."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:253
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>Ssh</title>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:255
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Click button <guibutton>Add</guibutton>, select rule type <guimenuitem>"
"Simple</guimenuitem>, and then fill in:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literallayout
#: index.docbook:257
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Policy: Allow;\n"
"Direction: Incoming;\n"
"Port: Secure Shell."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:259
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As for the logging, you can choose to log <guimenuitem>"
"New connections</guimenuitem>. The <guilabel>Description</guilabel> "
"field is optional, and useful only when you enter some rule including port "
"numbers and IP addresses that you can forget the meaning of."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:261
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Once you have done all of this, click <guibutton>Add</guibutton>"
". Now you have the first rule in place! The dialog remains open so you can "
"start filling the next rule right away."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:267
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding SSH rule"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:275
#, no-c-format
msgid "Dropbox"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:278
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To add Dropbox LAN sync service to the list, look at the list of pre-defined "
"services in the dialog. It is not there. So you will have to find out "
"yourself "
"which port needs to be opened for Dropbox to function properly. Just google "
"for "
"\"Dropbox LAN sync port\", and you will find that it is 17500 TCP/UDP ports "
"that need to be opened. Alternatively, you can get the ports needed for a "
"program by starting the program and then running"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: index.docbook:281
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<prompt>$</prompt> <command>sudo netstat -ap | grep dropbox | grep \"*:*\" | "
"grep -iE \"(TCP|UDP)\"</command>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:284
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"in the &konsole; window (change dropbox to the program you are investigating)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:286
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To open Dropbox LAN sync ports, click the button <guibutton>Add</guibutton>"
", select the type <guimenuitem>Simple</guimenuitem>, and fill in:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literallayout
#: index.docbook:288
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Policy: Allow;\n"
"Direction: Incoming;\n"
"Protocol: Any;\n"
"Port: 17500."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:290
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Then select the desired logging level, enter a description that will help you "
"recognize the rule in the future, like \"Dropbox LAN sync\" and add the rule."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:296
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding Dropbox LAN sync rule"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:300
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Now, you can also get extra security by restricting the access to the Dropbox "
"LAN sync port to only your laptop, which, remember, is constantly given the "
"same IP address 192.168.1.111 by the router. So, you can choose to limit "
"access "
"to that particular IP address. In that case, choose the rule type <"
"guimenuitem>"
"Advanced</guimenuitem>, and fill in:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literallayout
#: index.docbook:302
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Policy: Allow; \n"
"Direction: Incoming;\n"
"Protocol: Any;\n"
"Source: [Address] 192.168.1.111: [Port] 17500;\n"
"Description: \"Dropbox LAN sync / one address\""
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:308
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding more secure Dropbox LAN sync rule"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:316
#, no-c-format
msgid "Samba"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:319
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To add Samba, go the same route as with ssh. Make a new rule of type "
"<guimenuitem>Simple</guimenuitem> and fill in:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literallayout
#: index.docbook:321
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Policy: Allow; \n"
"Direction: Incoming;\n"
"Port: Samba;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:327
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding Samba rule"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:331
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Now, Samba is a service that likes to open quite random ports on each "
"connection, so you will probably need to enable advanced functionality in the "
"firewall – &ie; the NetBIOS (which is a subset of Samba) kernel module. To do "
"so, click the <guilabel>Modules</guilabel> tab and check the check box "
"<guilabel>Connection Tracking</guilabel> for NetBIOS. For an explanation of "
"what a module does, see the <link linkend=\"modules\">Modules section</link> "
"of this handbook."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:337
#, no-c-format
msgid "Enabling the NetBios kernel module (for Samba file sharing)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:342
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can also further modify the rules that were added for Samba, to make your "
"Samba shares only possible to access from your notebook computer. In order to "
"do that, you need to open the two rules that were created by the previous "
"action (separate rules were created for TCP and for the UDP ports), change "
"the "
"rule type to <guimenuitem>Advanced</guimenuitem>, and enter the IP address of "
"your laptop (192.168.1.111) to the <guilabel>Source [Address]</guilabel> "
"field (just like we did with Dropbox LAN sync rule, only here we have to "
"modify "
"two rules)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:348
#, no-c-format
msgid "KTorrent"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:350
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To have KTorrent operational, you will have to add a new rule of type "
"\"Simple\" and fill in:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: literallayout
#: index.docbook:352
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Policy: Allow;\n"
"Direction: Incoming;\n"
"Port: KTorrent;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:359
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding ktorrent rule"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:363
#, no-c-format
msgid "Two rules for TCP and UDP ports will be added as the result."
msgstr ""

